>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>How to Cite the Library</h1>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is built to be cited — in court filings,
      in legislative testimony, in scholarship, and in journalism. We want to
      be cited. We ask only that the citation be accurate, so a reader who
      follows it lands on the same page we did and can verify the underlying
      source for themselves. This page explains how.
    </p>

    <h2>General form</h2>

    <p>
      The Library is the publisher. The work you are citing — a Reading Room
      essay, a state catalog page, a Counting House figure page, or one of the
      book-length works the Library publishes — is what the citation names.
      Where the page lists a named author, that author goes first. The URL is
      the locator. A reader needs the date you visited the page, because the
      Library is a living catalog and pages do get revised.
    </p>

    <p>The general form, modeled on standard digital-library practice:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Author Last, First. "Page Title." <em>America's School Trust Library</em>,
        accessed YYYY-MM-DD, https://schooltrusts.org/path/.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Two worked examples:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Sullivan, Dave. <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>. America's School Trust Library,
        accessed 2026-08-12, https://schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Oregon." <em>America's School Trust Library</em>, accessed 2026-08-12,
        https://schooltrusts.org/reading/us-or/.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The first example is one of the book-length works the Library publishes,
      so its title is italicized and the Library is named as its publisher. The
      second has no author line because state catalog pages are institutional
      work — there is no individual byline on the page, so the citation begins
      with the page title, and a page inside the Library takes quotation marks
      around its title with the Library italicized as the containing work.
    </p>

    <h2>In specific styles</h2>

    <p>
      The form above is close to several common styles already. Here is the
      same work rendered four ways.
    </p>

    <h3>MLA</h3>

    <p>
      Sullivan, Dave. <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>. America's School Trust Library,
      2026, schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/. Accessed 12 Aug. 2026.
    </p>

    <h3>Chicago — notes and bibliography</h3>

    <p>
      <em>Note:</em> Dave Sullivan, <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>, America's School
      Trust Library, accessed August 12, 2026,
      https://schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>Bibliography:</em> Sullivan, Dave. <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>.
      America's School Trust Library. Accessed August 12, 2026.
      https://schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/.
    </p>

    <h3>Chicago — author-date</h3>

    <p>
      Sullivan, Dave. 2026. <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>. America's School Trust
      Library. Accessed August 12, 2026.
      https://schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/.
    </p>

    <h3>Bluebook (for legal writing)</h3>

    <p>
      Dave Sullivan, <em>Stewards of the Republic</em>, AMERICA'S SCHOOL TRUST
      LIBRARY (Aug. 12, 2026),
      https://schooltrusts.org/writing/stewards-of-the-republic/.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a state catalog page in a brief: <em>Oregon</em>, AMERICA'S SCHOOL
      TRUST LIBRARY (Aug. 12, 2026), https://schooltrusts.org/reading/us-or/.
    </p>

    <h2>Citing primary sources reached through the Library</h2>

    <p>
      When the Library hosts or links a statute, a court filing, or a historical
      document, cite the primary source directly — the statute citation, the
      reporter citation, the archival call number — and name the Library as the
      access point.
    </p>

    <p>
      So a statute reached through the Library is cited to its code, then noted
      as reached through the Library:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Or. Admis. Act of 1859, 11 Stat. 383, § 4 (reached through <em>America's
        School Trust Library</em> at
        https://schooltrusts.org/reading/library/oregon-admission-act-1859/).
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>A court filing hosted on the Library:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Letter Op. Granting Summ. J., <em>Siuslaw Sch. Dist. 97J v. State</em>,
        No. 24CV38372 (Or. Cir. Ct. Coos Cnty. July 24, 2026) (available via
        <em>America's School Trust Library</em> at
        https://schooltrusts.org/court/filings/2026-07-24-combs-summary-judgment-letter-24cv38372.pdf).
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The reader who needs to verify the statute looks it up in the published
      code; the reader who needs the court filing finds it where we hosted it.
      Both readers know exactly where to look.
    </p>

    <h2>Citing figures from the Counting House</h2>

    <p>
      Every figure in the Counting House carries a confidence badge, an
      as-of date or fiscal year, and the date the record was last reviewed.
      What it does not yet carry is a published source line for each figure:
      those fields are empty in the substrate today. Until they are filled,
      cite the underlying source — the statute, audit, or agency report the
      number came from — where you have it, and treat the Counting House page
      as the access point. Where you do not have it, cite the Counting House
      page and say what it told you.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <em>America's School Trust Library</em>, Counting House, Oregon,
        https://schooltrusts.org/counting/us-or/ (permanent fund corpus
        $2 billion as of June 30, 2025; record last reviewed April 29, 2026).
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The principle: a number's authority comes from the source the number is
      drawn from, not from the page that displays it. Where the Library cannot
      yet name that source, the citation should say so rather than borrow
      authority the page does not have.
    </p>

    <h2>Permission and reuse</h2>

    <p>
      Catalog text and Reading Room essays written by the Library are available
      for quotation under standard fair-use principles for scholarship,
      journalism, court filings, and legislative work. Quote what you need;
      cite what you quote. Quoting a Reading Room essay at length, or
      republishing a page in whole or substantial part, requires written
      permission. Contact details are on the <a href="/about/">About page</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Material the Library hosts but did not write — statutes, court filings,
      archival documents, third-party photographs — is governed by the rights
      that attach to the original source. For takedown procedure and the full
      rights notice, see <a href="/permissions/">Rights, Use, and Takedown</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>If a citation looks wrong</h2>

    <p>
      Accuracy is the only thing we ask of the citing reader, and accuracy is
      also what we ask of ourselves. If you follow a citation and the page no
      longer says what we said it said, or if a figure has changed since you
      cited it, use the Submit-Correction control at the foot of the page in
      question. We will look at it, fix it if it needs fixing, and note the
      change. Citations that point to a moving target serve no one; we work
      hard to keep the targets steady. For the full correction protocol, see
      <a href="/about/corrections/">Corrections</a>.
    </p>
